# 1️⃣ Bellman Ford Algorithm

## Purpose

Find shortest path from:

```text
Single Source
```

to:

```text
All Vertices
```

Works even when:

```text
Negative Edge Weights Exist
```

---

### Example

```text
0 → 1 = 4

0 → 2 = 5

1 → 2 = -2
```

Bellman Ford works perfectly.

---

# 2️⃣ Core Idea

Relax every edge:

```text
(V - 1) times
```

because shortest path can contain at most:

```text
(V - 1) edges
```

---

# 3️⃣ Relaxation Formula

```cpp
if(
dist[u] + wt
<
dist[v]
)
{
    dist[v] =
    dist[u] + wt;
}
```

---

# 4️⃣ Bellman Ford Code

```cpp
vector<int> bellmanFord(
    int V,
    vector<vector<int>>& edges,
    int src)
{
    vector<int> dist(
        V,
        1e8
    );

    dist[src] = 0;

    for(int i = 1;
        i <= V - 1;
        i++)
    {
        for(auto edge : edges)
        {
            int u = edge[0];
            int v = edge[1];
            int wt = edge[2];

            if(
                dist[u] != 1e8 &&
                dist[u] + wt <
                dist[v]
            )
            {
                dist[v] =
                    dist[u] + wt;
            }
        }
    }

    return dist;
}
```

---

### Complexity

```text
Time: O(V × E)

Space: O(V)
```

---

# 5️⃣ Negative Cycle Detection

After:

```text
V - 1
```

relaxations,

perform:

```text
One More Relaxation
```

If distance still changes:

```text
Negative Cycle Exists
```

